WEBVTT

00:00.216 --> 00:01.465
- [Instructor] So we're
given a series here,

00:01.465 --> 00:03.026
and they say, "What series should we use

00:03.026 --> 00:04.994
"in the limit comparison test?"

00:04.994 --> 00:07.379
Let me underline that,
"The limit comparison test,

00:07.379 --> 00:10.559
"in order to determine
whether S converges?"

00:10.559 --> 00:12.088
So let's just remind ourselves

00:12.088 --> 00:13.979
about the limit comparison test.

00:13.979 --> 00:18.490
If we say, if we say
that we have two series,

00:18.490 --> 00:21.429
and I'll just use this notation, a sub-n,

00:21.429 --> 00:24.179
and then another series, b sub-n.

00:25.107 --> 00:29.953
And we know that a sub-n
and b sub-n are greater than

00:29.953 --> 00:34.120
or equal to zero for all n,
for all n, if we know this,

00:36.985 --> 00:41.152
then if, then if the limit
as n approaches infinity of

00:45.154 --> 00:49.321
a sub-n over b sub-n is equal
to some positive constant.

00:51.119 --> 00:55.110
So zero is less than that
constant is less than infinity,

00:55.110 --> 00:58.943
then either both converge,
then both converge,

01:01.263 --> 01:03.680
both converge, both converge,

01:06.411 --> 01:08.911
or both diverge, both diverge.

01:11.497 --> 01:13.316
And it really makes a lot of
sense, because it's saying,

01:13.316 --> 01:16.555
look, as we get into our
really large values of n,

01:16.555 --> 01:19.230
as we go really far out
there in terms of the terms,

01:19.230 --> 01:22.759
if our behavior starts to look
the same, then it makes sense

01:22.759 --> 01:25.310
that both these series
would converge or diverge,

01:25.310 --> 01:27.262
and we have an introductory
vizio, (chuckles)

01:27.262 --> 01:30.515
we have an introductory video
on this in another video.

01:30.515 --> 01:33.081
So let's think about what,
if we say that this is our

01:33.081 --> 01:37.582
a sub-n, if we say that this
is a sub-n right over here,

01:37.582 --> 01:40.604
what is a series that we
can really compare to?

01:40.604 --> 01:45.119
That seems to have the same
behavior as n gets really large?

01:45.119 --> 01:47.603
Well this one just
seems to gets unbounded.

01:47.603 --> 01:49.581
This one doesn't look that similar.

01:49.581 --> 01:51.814
Has a three to the n minus
one in the denominator,

01:51.814 --> 01:54.716
but the numerator doesn't behave the same.

01:54.716 --> 01:56.541
This one over here is interesting

01:56.541 --> 01:58.316
because we could write this,

01:58.316 --> 02:02.433
the is the same as a sum
n equals one to infinity.

02:02.433 --> 02:06.426
We could write this is two
to the n over three to the n,

02:06.426 --> 02:07.689
and these are very similar.

02:07.689 --> 02:10.368
The only difference between
this and this is that

02:10.368 --> 02:12.705
in the denominator here, or
in the denominator up here

02:12.705 --> 02:14.841
we have a minus one, and down here

02:14.841 --> 02:16.738
we don't have that minus one,

02:16.738 --> 02:19.054
and so it makes sense given
that that's just a constant,

02:19.054 --> 02:23.063
that as n gets very large, that
these might behave the same.

02:23.063 --> 02:24.202
So let's try it out.

02:24.202 --> 02:25.952
Let's find the limit,

02:27.984 --> 02:30.705
and we also know that the a
sub-n's and the b sub-n's,

02:30.705 --> 02:33.275
if we say that this over here is b sub-n,

02:33.275 --> 02:37.932
we say that's b sub-n, that
this is going to be positive,

02:37.932 --> 02:39.997
or this is going to be
greater than or equal to zero

02:39.997 --> 02:42.779
for n equals one, two,
three, so for any values

02:42.779 --> 02:46.176
this is going to be greater
than or equal to zero,

02:46.176 --> 02:48.454
and the same thing right over here.

02:48.454 --> 02:50.119
It's gonna be greater
than or equal to zero

02:50.119 --> 02:51.999
for all of the n's that we care about.

02:51.999 --> 02:54.907
So we meet these first
constraints, and so let's find

02:54.907 --> 02:58.435
the limit as n approaches
infinity of a sub-n,

02:58.435 --> 03:00.654
which is, I'll write in that red color,

03:00.654 --> 03:04.821
which is two to the nth power
over three to the n minus one

03:07.178 --> 03:11.345
over b sub-n, over two to the
nth over three to the nth.

03:14.070 --> 03:15.855
So let me actually do a little

03:15.855 --> 03:17.539
algebraic manipulation right over here.

03:17.539 --> 03:19.735
This is going to to be the same thing as

03:19.735 --> 03:23.485
two to the nth over
three to the n minus one,

03:24.416 --> 03:27.499
times three to the n, over two the n.

03:29.910 --> 03:31.646
Divide the numerator and the denominators

03:31.646 --> 03:34.034
by two to the n, those cancel out,

03:34.034 --> 03:38.438
and so this will give
us, this will give us

03:38.438 --> 03:42.506
three to the n over
three to the n minus one.

03:42.506 --> 03:44.366
We can divide the numerator
and the denominator

03:44.366 --> 03:46.167
by three to the n, and that'll give us

03:46.167 --> 03:49.750
one over one minus one
over three to the n.

03:50.951 --> 03:53.896
So we could say this is
the same thing as the limit

03:53.896 --> 03:58.063
as n approaches infinity
of one over one minus one,

03:59.824 --> 04:01.537
over three to the n.

04:01.537 --> 04:03.360
Well what's this going to be equal to?

04:03.360 --> 04:05.149
Well as that approaches
infinity, this thing,

04:05.149 --> 04:07.416
one over three to the n,
that's just gonna go to zero.

04:07.416 --> 04:10.994
So this is, this whole thing
is just going to approach one.

04:10.994 --> 04:14.330
And one is clearly
between zero and infinity,

04:14.330 --> 04:17.003
so the destinies of these
two series are tied.

04:17.003 --> 04:21.286
They either both converge
or they both diverge.

04:21.286 --> 04:25.315
So this is a good one to use
a limit comparison test with,

04:25.315 --> 04:26.177
and so let's think about it.

04:26.177 --> 04:29.809
Do they either both converge,
or do they both diverge?

04:29.809 --> 04:33.541
Well this is a geometric
series, our common ratio here

04:33.541 --> 04:37.336
is less than one, so this
is going to converge,

04:37.336 --> 04:38.750
this is going to converge.

04:38.750 --> 04:40.217
And because this one converges, by the

04:40.217 --> 04:45.031
limit comparison test, our
original series S converges.

04:45.031 --> 04:47.281
Converges, and we are done.
